<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-is-it-a-map" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Is it a map?
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Consider the sets <m> X = \set{ 0 , 2 , 4 , 6 , 8 } </m> and <m> Y = \set{ 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } </m>. Determine whether or not the given object is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether or not
        
        <md>
          f = \set{ \of{ 0 , 0 } , \of{ 2 , 1 } , \of{ 4 , 2 } , \of{ 6 , 3 } , \of{ 8 , 4 } }
        </md>
        
        is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        Yes, <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether or not
        
        <md>
          g = \set{ \of{ 0 , 1 } , \of{ 2 , 1 } , \of{ 4 , 4 } , \of{ 8 , 1 } }
        </md>
        
        is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        No, <m> g </m> is not a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        There is no output <m> y \in Y </m> so that <m> \of{ 6 , y } \in g </m>, and so <m> g </m> does not satisfy the totality condition. Thus <m> g </m> is not a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether or not
        
        <md>
          h = \set{ \of{ 0 , 1 } , \of{ 2 , 1 } , \of{ 4 , 4 } , \of{ 4 , 3 } , \of{ 6 , 2 } , \of{ 8 , 1 } }
        </md>
        
        is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        No, <m> h </m> is not a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Note that <m> \of{ 4 , 4 } , \of{ 4 , 3 } \in h </m>, and so <m> h </m> does not satisfy the uniqueness condition. Thus <m> h </m> is not a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether or not
        
        <md>
          j = \set{ \of{ 0 , 0 } , \of{ 2 , 0 } , \of{ 4 , 0 } , \of{ 6 , 0 } , \of{ 8 , 0 } }
        </md>
        
        is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        Yes, <m> j \colon X \to Y </m> is a map from <m> X </m> to <m> Y </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
